1
00:00:00,750 --> 00:00:06,442
Right now, the screen you are watching this on runs on electricity that traveled from very far away.

2
00:00:06,642 --> 00:00:12,565
And along the way it went back and forth: changing direction a hundred times per second, or more.

3
00:00:12,765 --> 00:00:18,304
All of that began in a park, at sunset, with a man reciting a poem.

4
00:00:18,504 --> 00:00:23,350
Suddenly he stopped, picked up a small stick, and drew something in the sand.

5
00:00:24,250 --> 00:00:27,327
That drawing still makes the world spin today.

6
00:00:27,877 --> 00:00:33,031
This is the story of Nikola Tesla. And how light reaches your home.

7
00:00:33,881 --> 00:00:39,573
Tesla was born in 1856, in a mountain village in present-day Croatia.

8
00:00:39,793 --> 00:00:49,870
Family legend says there was a storm that night, and the midwife called him a child of the storm. His mother corrected her: no, of light.

9
00:00:50,070 --> 00:00:57,455
As a boy he saw sparks when petting his cat in the dark. His father told him it was electricity, just like lightning.

10
00:00:57,655 --> 00:01:03,116
And he could imagine a machine and run it inside his head, without ever building it.

11
00:01:03,316 --> 00:01:10,316
At university he saw a motor throwing sparks everywhere. He said you could build one without sparks.

12
00:01:10,516 --> 00:01:14,747
The professor explained to him, in front of everyone, that it was impossible.

13
00:01:15,647 --> 00:01:19,262
Tesla spent years trying to prove the opposite.

14
00:01:20,112 --> 00:01:26,497
Years later, in Budapest, he was still so obsessed with that motor that he fell ill.

15
00:01:26,717 --> 00:01:33,255
One afternoon he went for a walk with a friend. He was reciting Goethe's Faust, which he knew by heart.

16
00:01:33,455 --> 00:01:40,840
As he told it, the idea struck him all at once, like a bolt of lightning. With a small stick, he drew the motor in the sand.

17
00:01:41,290 --> 00:01:44,367
To understand what he saw, let's go to the chalkboard.

18
00:01:44,567 --> 00:01:50,336
Direct current always pushes the same way, like water flowing through a pipe.

19
00:01:50,536 --> 00:01:56,459
Alternating current goes back and forth, like a handsaw: it doesn't move forward, yet it still cuts the wood.

20
00:01:56,659 --> 00:02:05,428
Tesla combined two alternating currents, one slightly delayed behind the other. Together they create an invisible spinning magnet.

21
00:02:05,628 --> 00:02:10,398
Put a piece of metal in the middle and it chases it. It spins without sparks.

22
00:02:11,298 --> 00:02:16,682
The impossible motor was already working. For now, only in his head.

23
00:02:17,532 --> 00:02:25,071
In 1884 he arrived in New York to work for the most famous inventor in the world: Thomas Edison.

24
00:02:25,291 --> 00:02:36,752
Edison lit the city with direct current, and his entire business depended on it. According to Tesla, he was promised fifty thousand dollars if he improved their generators.

25
00:02:36,952 --> 00:02:44,491
He worked for months barely sleeping. When he went to collect, they told him it had been a joke. He quit.

26
00:02:44,691 --> 00:02:53,460
Tesla started his own company and his partners pushed him out. He ended up digging ditches in the street for two dollars a day.

27
00:02:53,660 --> 00:02:58,583
The foreman heard him talking about his motor and introduced him to a group of investors.

28
00:02:58,783 --> 00:03:04,322
He told them about the Egg of Columbus, who according to legend stood an egg upright by tapping its tip.

29
00:03:04,522 --> 00:03:11,598
Tesla placed a copper egg on the table, switched on his current… and the egg spun until it stood upright.

30
00:03:11,798 --> 00:03:18,568
It was his invisible magnet, spinning beneath the wood. They put up the money. Remember that egg.

31
00:03:19,468 --> 00:03:24,006
Columbus had to crack the egg. Tesla made it dance.

32
00:03:24,856 --> 00:03:30,318
Word of his motor reached George Westinghouse, an industrialist who was betting on alternating current.

33
00:03:30,538 --> 00:03:38,615
He paid to use his patents and promised him two dollars and fifty cents for every horsepower sold. Remember that number.

34
00:03:39,065 --> 00:03:41,988
Why such a fierce battle? Back to the chalkboard.

35
00:03:42,188 --> 00:03:55,495
Wires lose energy as heat. The more current flows through them, the more they lose. But the same energy can travel as lots of current at low voltage… or very little current at high voltage.

36
00:03:55,695 --> 00:04:03,003
With a transformer, alternating current steps up the voltage to travel far. And steps it down before entering your home.

37
00:04:03,203 --> 00:04:09,972
Direct current back then couldn't do that. It needed a power station every mile or so.

38
00:04:10,172 --> 00:04:17,403
Edison's camp responded with fear: they electrocuted animals in public to show that alternating current killed.

39
00:04:17,603 --> 00:04:31,142
Tesla answered with a spectacle: he let ultra-high-frequency currents pass through his body and lit lamps with his bare hands. That only worked at extreme frequencies, and even then it was dangerous.

40
00:04:32,042 --> 00:04:37,580
Edison said alternating current killed. Tesla used it to light up his hands.

41
00:04:38,430 --> 00:04:47,199
In 1893, Chicago hosted a World's Fair. It was going to be the first ever lit entirely by electricity.

42
00:04:47,419 --> 00:04:54,188
Westinghouse won the contract: he offered to do it with alternating current for far less than Edison's camp.

43
00:04:54,388 --> 00:05:01,235
On opening day, the president pressed a button… and about a hundred thousand lamps lit up.

44
00:05:01,435 --> 00:05:08,358
Inside the Electricity Building, crowds gathered in front of a giant copper egg. It spun all by itself.

45
00:05:08,558 --> 00:05:15,558
A few years later, his system carried power from Niagara Falls to Buffalo, over twenty miles away.

46
00:05:15,758 --> 00:05:23,065
But the war left Westinghouse buried in debt. His bankers wanted to eliminate Tesla's two dollars and fifty cents.

47
00:05:23,265 --> 00:05:28,958
The most famous version says Tesla tore up the contract right in front of him, out of friendship.

48
00:05:29,158 --> 00:05:38,312
The documents tell a less theatrical story: he gave up his royalties and later sold his patents for about two hundred and sixteen thousand dollars.

49
00:05:39,212 --> 00:05:44,519
His system conquered the world. And he no longer collected a cent from it.

50
00:05:45,369 --> 00:05:52,908
In his New York laboratory, Tesla became a celebrity. One of his friends was the writer Mark Twain.

51
00:05:53,128 --> 00:05:59,974
They say he once invited him onto a vibrating platform. Before long, Twain went running to the bathroom.

52
00:06:00,174 --> 00:06:06,020
His signature tool was the Tesla coil. It works on something you've known since childhood.

53
00:06:06,470 --> 00:06:14,008
If you push a swing right as it comes back, gentle pushes send it higher and higher. That is resonance.

54
00:06:14,208 --> 00:06:21,747
The coil pushes electricity at the exact right moment. That's how it achieves huge voltages and multi-meter sparks.

55
00:06:21,947 --> 00:06:28,332
At an exhibition in New York, he placed an iron boat in a pool. With nobody inside.

56
00:06:28,532 --> 00:06:35,762
The audience shouted questions and the boat answered with its lights. Some believed he was controlling it with his mind.

57
00:06:35,962 --> 00:06:42,885
He was steering it with radio waves from a small box. It is the first radio-controlled device on record.

58
00:06:43,785 --> 00:06:48,247
Today we call it a remote control. And we use it to change the channel.

59
00:06:49,097 --> 00:06:56,712
Tesla wanted more. In Colorado he built a giant coil and produced lightning bolts over a hundred feet long.

60
00:06:56,932 --> 00:07:05,009
One night he burned out the power company's generator… and the town went dark. He had to pay for the repairs.

61
00:07:05,209 --> 00:07:13,209
That's where his most famous photo comes from, reading calmly beside giant lightning bolts. It's a trick: two photos superimposed.

62
00:07:13,409 --> 00:07:20,102
Later, banker J. P. Morgan gave him a hundred and fifty thousand dollars to build a tower.

63
00:07:20,302 --> 00:07:28,532
Morgan wanted wireless messages. Tesla dreamed of something bigger: wireless power for the entire planet.

64
00:07:28,732 --> 00:07:35,886
But Marconi crossed the Atlantic with a radio signal and simpler equipment. Morgan put up no more money.

65
00:07:36,336 --> 00:07:45,490
The tower never worked. And physics wasn't helping: wireless energy spreads out in every direction, like light from a lamp.

66
00:07:45,690 --> 00:07:51,459
The farther you go, the less arrives. Across a few centimeters, however, it works very well.

67
00:07:52,359 --> 00:07:58,128
His wireless energy exists. It reaches a few centimeters and charges your phone.

68
00:07:58,978 --> 00:08:09,286
In his final years he lived in New York hotels. He spent his nights feeding the pigeons. He said he loved one white pigeon like no other.

69
00:08:09,506 --> 00:08:18,583
Engineers awarded him their highest prize: the Edison Medal. According to his biographer, in the middle of the ceremony he vanished.

70
00:08:18,783 --> 00:08:22,552
They found him in a plaza, covered in pigeons.

71
00:08:22,752 --> 00:08:31,060
He died alone in his hotel room in 1943. He was eighty-six years old and had almost no money.

72
00:08:31,560 --> 00:08:43,868
Since 1960, his last name is a unit: the tesla. A hospital MRI scanner uses between 1.5 and 3. A fridge magnet, a few thousandths.

73
00:08:44,068 --> 00:08:49,683
And his induction motors keep spinning in factories, water pumps, and fans.

74
00:08:49,883 --> 00:08:55,422
While you watch this, the current in your home goes back and forth, pushed by his idea.

75
00:08:55,622 --> 00:09:02,929
The night he was born, the midwife said he would be a child of the storm. His mother said no: of light.

76
00:09:03,879 --> 00:09:05,572
They were both right.
